(1851544, 251383, 17507, '<td valign="top">bah, this should have finished over a 6. It could have benefited from some adjustments in lighting (I think the spotlight would be best directly behind the girl), but that''s fairly minor - the absurdity combined with the girl''s natural-looking smile and the water bottle should have been better recognized and rewarded.</td>', 'bah, this should have finished over a 6. It could have benefited from some adjustments in lighting (I think the spotlight would be best directly behind the girl), but that''s fairly minor - the absurdity combined with the girl''s natural-looking smile and the water bottle should have been better recognized and rewarded.', '2005-11-09 15:08:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(1851702, 250775, 24962, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Colour Abandoned was your entry in the Transparency II challenge and it is one serious photo.<br/> <br/>Technical:<br/>Good use of the maximum permitted size, beautifully focused and good DOF. The desat works superbly and there is no sign of oversharpening, although there appears to be a hint of noise/grain in the frame, however this adds to the overall effect. I could be really picking and start inspecting ever detail, but I won''t because the image stands as it is.<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/>I like the positioning, it is not quite central in that big square format which makes it feel even more quirky. The reflections in the glass look ghostly and fit the abandoned house very well. My only criticism is that the bottom of the window frame appears to be resting on the ground. I know it isn''t, but that is the impression I get, due to the rising ground behind and the long grass. But it doesn''t spoil the image in anyway.<br/> <br/>Overall:<br/>A stunning image with great imagination. I can''t find any faults, other than my brief mention of the hanging bit, and that isn''t a fault. Your past images are well scoring and this 7th place is well deserved. A great image for prints. Well Done and I hope this is helpful and constructive.<br/> <br/>Steve</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: Colour Abandoned was your entry in the Transparency II challenge and it is one serious photo. Technical:Good use of the maximum permitted size, beautifully focused and good DOF. The desat works superbly and there is no sign of oversharpening, although there appears to be a hint of noise/grain in the frame, however this adds to the overall effect. I could be really picking and start inspecting ever detail, but I won''t because the image stands as it is. Composition:I like the positioning, it is not quite central in that big square format which makes it feel even more quirky. The reflections in the glass look ghostly and fit the abandoned house very well. My only criticism is that the bottom of the window frame appears to be resting on the ground. I know it isn''t, but that is the impression I get, due to the rising ground behind and the long grass. But it doesn''t spoil the image in anyway. Overall:A stunning image with great imagination. I can''t find any faults, other than my brief mention of the hanging bit, and that isn''t a fault. Your past images are well scoring and this 7th place is well deserved. A great image for prints. Well Done and I hope this is helpful and constructive. Steve', '2005-11-09 15:44:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1851985, 251485, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi Funnylooks...<br/> <br/>I read your bio on your profile before I started this critique to get a little insight into your interests.  First of all, I must apologize for not being a good source of critique on an image of this nature.  It''s a bit outside of my own style and philosophies.  I think the concept here is worthwhile but it may need a little refinement to achieve a photo that you, as well as the general public, will find intriguing on a higher level.  I think as far as DPChallenge is concerned, the highlights caught by the light source and the plastic bag may be one of the primary reasons this image didn''t score higher.  "Conceptual" photography is a tough arena when you are being judged by a very diverse demographic.  In your circles of friend on a college campus, this photo would likely have acheived a much higher rating.  It would be more in tune with the pop art and culture that thrives in those groups.  Everyone around here talks about ''getting outside the box'' in thinking and preparation for challenges, but I really don''t think that being outside the box is the answer for this type of contest in most cases.  With the number and diversity of people here, I believe that simple and straightforward images will outperform others in most cases.  Any image that requires more than a few seconds of viewing and thought will get tossed aside during a vote.  <br/> <br/>I wish you well in your photogrpahy and look forward to seeing more in the future :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi Funnylooks... I read your bio on your profile before I started this critique to get a little insight into your interests.  First of all, I must apologize for not being a good source of critique on an image of this nature.  It''s a bit outside of my own style and philosophies.  I think the concept here is worthwhile but it may need a little refinement to achieve a photo that you, as well as the general public, will find intriguing on a higher level.  I think as far as DPChallenge is concerned, the highlights caught by the light source and the plastic bag may be one of the primary reasons this image didn''t score higher.  "Conceptual" photography is a tough arena when you are being judged by a very diverse demographic.  In your circles of friend on a college campus, this photo would likely have acheived a much higher rating.  It would be more in tune with the pop art and culture that thrives in those groups.  Everyone around here talks about ''getting outside the box'' in thinking and preparation for challenges, but I really don''t think that being outside the box is the answer for this type of contest in most cases.  With the number and diversity of people here, I believe that simple and straightforward images will outperform others in most cases.  Any image that requires more than a few seconds of viewing and thought will get tossed aside during a vote.   I wish you well in your photogrpahy and look forward to seeing more in the future :) John Setzler ', '2005-11-09 16:58:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852028, 250994, 42984, '<td valign="top"><br/>The commenters have said it all really.<br/> <br/>I would have preferred to have seen you Photographer''s Comments with your submission so we could get a sense of what you were trying to do<br/> <br/>Brett<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.</i></td>', 'The commenters have said it all really. I would have preferred to have seen you Photographer''s Comments with your submission so we could get a sense of what you were trying to do BrettMessage edited by HBunch - Removed Critique Club status.', '2005-11-09 17:11:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852037, 251493, 42984, '<td valign="top">::: cRITIQUE cLUB :::<br/> <br/>Nice Product Shot. <br/> <br/>To really push transparency, you probably needed to work some backlight into this shot.  Your subject has ended up more opaque than transparent.<br/> <br/>I can''t really do a complete critique for you as there are no Photographer''s Comments with your submission which would give us a sense of what you were trying to achieve and how.<br/> <br/>Brett</td>', '::: cRITIQUE cLUB ::: Nice Product Shot.  To really push transparency, you probably needed to work some backlight into this shot.  Your subject has ended up more opaque than transparent. I can''t really do a complete critique for you as there are no Photographer''s Comments with your submission which would give us a sense of what you were trying to achieve and how. Brett', '2005-11-09 17:15:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852079, 249993, 18552, '<td valign="top">I really like this picture, it really isn''t dull at all. The lighting suits the mood of the picture. Sometimes, really good pictures don''t do well just because they don''t appeal to the masses - I gave your picture a 7 in the challenge - I like it a lot. The compostition is very good and I like the delicate quality of the shot, it goes well with the bottles.</td>', 'I really like this picture, it really isn''t dull at all. The lighting suits the mood of the picture. Sometimes, really good pictures don''t do well just because they don''t appeal to the masses - I gave your picture a 7 in the challenge - I like it a lot. The compostition is very good and I like the delicate quality of the shot, it goes well with the bottles.', '2005-11-09 17:28:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852100, 250956, 24013, '<td valign="top">you rarely see a shot of northern lights that''s also this well composed.</td>', 'you rarely see a shot of northern lights that''s also this well composed.', '2005-11-09 17:32:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852175, 251267, 42984, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/>Good quality nature photography, especially macro, is a pleasure to view.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Stunningly clear and interesting.  The whole package is strong and draws you as viewer into the image to learn more. Even though I hate bugs, this is a cool photo. I didn''t see it as transparent.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>Normally, one is obliged to point out the thirds rule and suggest that a centred image, as this one is, can be less powerful than on off-centred one.  I''m not sure that is the case here since it looks balanced and well composed just as it is.  Part of that perception of balance may be because the subject itself has formed a leading line which then determines the point of interest at the head rather well.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>I don''t really see this as a record of transparency, which is probably where it lost most of its voting points.  Getting away from the challenge and concentrating on the photograph, it is a powerful and well crafted image of a praying mantis.  I could see that as an illustration in a book quite easily.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>The real power of this image is in its colouring.  Image how it would have looked if there''d bee red or yellow flowers behind the mantis. It is the green subject on green background (the same greeens too) which give it such a significant look.<br/> <br/>Your lighting, focus and DOF are superb.  I''m drawn to the little folds on the lower neck.  The 90 deg to the left lighting is just perfect for this.  The shine on the foreleg gives us a feeling for the texture of the skin yet the parts in shadow are still quite clear and detailed.<br/> <br/>The light catching the eye is just plain spooky. When you look really hard, you can see the transparency of the eye, it''s just not expected or believable at first glance - which is a shame.<br/> <br/><b>To get a Ribbon?:</b> <br/>This is a fine bug shot.  Bug shots don''t get ribbons, they''re not meant to.  They are there to inform and involve, which is an entirely different brief.  As an insect study its a 10.  To get closer to a transparency ribbon then I suspect you have to go for the drama and impact of the transparent eye.<br/> <br/>I don''t know how much is cropped already or how many megapixels you have to work with but a head-only crop would be pretty scary and interesting. It would have serious drama and would meet the challenge in a way that few other submissions did.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>I didn''t ''like'' this image when I was voting, I only gave it a 5. I didn''t see the transparency of the eye, even though it was in the caption.  Without the ability to zoom in and have a look or darken the room, I missed the most important point of it.  Since it has come to me for critique and I have had the chance to work with it as an image outside of the challenge as well, I have come to really appreciate it.<br/> <br/>You did well<br/>Brett<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-11-09 18:03:05.</i></td>', '::: Critique Club ::: Good quality nature photography, especially macro, is a pleasure to view. First Impression - the most important one: Stunningly clear and interesting.  The whole package is strong and draws you as viewer into the image to learn more. Even though I hate bugs, this is a cool photo. I didn''t see it as transparent. Composition: Normally, one is obliged to point out the thirds rule and suggest that a centred image, as this one is, can be less powerful than on off-centred one.  I''m not sure that is the case here since it looks balanced and well composed just as it is.  Part of that perception of balance may be because the subject itself has formed a leading line which then determines the point of interest at the head rather well. Subject: I don''t really see this as a record of transparency, which is probably where it lost most of its voting points.  Getting away from the challenge and concentrating on the photograph, it is a powerful and well crafted image of a praying mantis.  I could see that as an illustration in a book quite easily. Technical (Colour and light): The real power of this image is in its colouring.  Image how it would have looked if there''d bee red or yellow flowers behind the mantis. It is the green subject on green background (the same greeens too) which give it such a significant look. Your lighting, focus and DOF are superb.  I''m drawn to the little folds on the lower neck.  The 90 deg to the left lighting is just perfect for this.  The shine on the foreleg gives us a feeling for the texture of the skin yet the parts in shadow are still quite clear and detailed. The light catching the eye is just plain spooky. When you look really hard, you can see the transparency of the eye, it''s just not expected or believable at first glance - which is a shame. To get a Ribbon?: This is a fine bug shot.  Bug shots don''t get ribbons, they''re not meant to.  They are there to inform and involve, which is an entirely different brief.  As an insect study its a 10.  To get closer to a transparency ribbon then I suspect you have to go for the drama and impact of the transparent eye. I don''t know how much is cropped already or how many megapixels you have to work with but a head-only crop would be pretty scary and interesting. It would have serious drama and would meet the challenge in a way that few other submissions did. Summary: I didn''t ''like'' this image when I was voting, I only gave it a 5. I didn''t see the transparency of the eye, even though it was in the caption.  Without the ability to zoom in and have a look or darken the room, I missed the most important point of it.  Since it has come to me for critique and I have had the chance to work with it as an image outside of the challenge as well, I have come to really appreciate it. You did wellBrett', '2005-11-09 17:49:14.000000', '2005-11-09 18:03:05.000000', 0),
(1852259, 250983, 43360, '<td valign="top">Fine, fine!</td>', 'Fine, fine!', '2005-11-09 18:13:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852298, 249523, 47487, '<td valign="top">Wait a minute, <i>isn''t</i> it a photograph underwater? If not, what is it then?</td>', 'Wait a minute, isn''t it a photograph underwater? If not, what is it then?', '2005-11-09 18:29:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852301, 250956, 45587, '<td valign="top">WOw!! magnificient!</td>', 'WOw!! magnificient!', '2005-11-09 18:30:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852309, 250983, 45587, '<td valign="top">Brilliant idea. Congrats.</td>', 'Brilliant idea. Congrats.', '2005-11-09 18:32:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852362, 250983, 28548, '<td valign="top">That''s awsome<br/></td>', 'That''s awsome', '2005-11-09 18:48:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852381, 250671, 19978, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>by strangeghost<br/> <br/><b>COMPOSITION</b> <br/>An intriguing image. The composition manages to convey a sense of business and loneliness at the same time. The subject is a bit difficult for me to make out though. Appears masculine for some reason (not sure exactly why I think that) but your title suggests it''s a woman. The passersby are ghostly and impersonal - maybe a bit too ghostly? Overall, I like the space and context - not too close, not too far. Just right. Excellent comp.<br/> <br/><b>TECHNIQUE</b> <br/>Here''s where your pic really gets interesting for me. Choosing the right shutter and aperture to capture the motion must be tricky, and I really like your result. The lighting has an orange caste that I  like a great deal. It really feels like a somewhat dirty street scene lit by those ghastly cheap sodium vapor lights that you find in many urban areas. Your use of shutter and aperture to capture the action is nearly perfect. I might have liked to see the ghostly walkers a have a bit more definition, but that''s splitting hairs in a very technically difficult shoot that you pulled off extremely well. Your depth of field is very great, which is a little surprising given your large aperture. I''m curious what lens you used, and whether it was a zoom or fixed.<br/> <br/><b>OVERALL IMPACT</b> <br/>Quite a lot of impact, IMO. I''m very happy to have had a chance to study this image closely. As I suggested above, there is a great deal of emotional tension here, implied abandonment, poverty, and the down-trodden ignored by the insensitive masses. It''s not easy to pack such a punch into a simple picture, but you''ve done it. Your final score of 5.7 is a bit disappointing in my view, but then it''s easy to see why many DPCers would pass pretty quickly by (nice irony there, no?) if they''re not immediately wowed by something.<br/> <br/>I just paused to read over your comments and am gratified to see that others recognized what I saw in this image as well. You should be proud of this accomplishment regardless of its final place in the challenge. I am honored to have been selected to critique it.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club by strangeghost COMPOSITION An intriguing image. The composition manages to convey a sense of business and loneliness at the same time. The subject is a bit difficult for me to make out though. Appears masculine for some reason (not sure exactly why I think that) but your title suggests it''s a woman. The passersby are ghostly and impersonal - maybe a bit too ghostly? Overall, I like the space and context - not too close, not too far. Just right. Excellent comp. TECHNIQUE Here''s where your pic really gets interesting for me. Choosing the right shutter and aperture to capture the motion must be tricky, and I really like your result. The lighting has an orange caste that I  like a great deal. It really feels like a somewhat dirty street scene lit by those ghastly cheap sodium vapor lights that you find in many urban areas. Your use of shutter and aperture to capture the action is nearly perfect. I might have liked to see the ghostly walkers a have a bit more definition, but that''s splitting hairs in a very technically difficult shoot that you pulled off extremely well. Your depth of field is very great, which is a little surprising given your large aperture. I''m curious what lens you used, and whether it was a zoom or fixed. OVERALL IMPACT Quite a lot of impact, IMO. I''m very happy to have had a chance to study this image closely. As I suggested above, there is a great deal of emotional tension here, implied abandonment, poverty, and the down-trodden ignored by the insensitive masses. It''s not easy to pack such a punch into a simple picture, but you''ve done it. Your final score of 5.7 is a bit disappointing in my view, but then it''s easy to see why many DPCers would pass pretty quickly by (nice irony there, no?) if they''re not immediately wowed by something. I just paused to read over your comments and am gratified to see that others recognized what I saw in this image as well. You should be proud of this accomplishment regardless of its final place in the challenge. I am honored to have been selected to critique it.', '2005-11-09 18:55:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852554, 250983, 25397, '<td valign="top">way to go muffin!</td>', 'way to go muffin!', '2005-11-09 19:51:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852690, 249432, 7543, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club Comment*<br/> <br/>Magnað, ég hef ekki fengið random mynd í þessum CC klúbb í marga mánuði og þegar ég geri það dett ég niður á þína mynd. <br/> <br/><b>Myndbygging</b> <br/>Fín bygging í myndinni, flott að fylla rammann. Eina sem fer í taugarnar á mér er að það rétt sést í nebbaling hægra megin, hefði e.t.v. mátt croppa nær glasinu eða vel inn í það. <br/> <br/><b>Lýsing</b> <br/>Fín lýsing hjá þér, Bowens gaurinn að skila sínu. Fer ekki í taugarnar á mér að myndist "yfirlýst" svæði á glasinu. Húðin kemur vel út og aðalatriðið, augað, er mjög flott. <br/> <br/><b>Tækni (e. camera work)</b> <br/>Mjög fín, kann vel við DOF í myndinni, hárið hæfilega blurrað eins og glasið og augað súper skarpt. <br/> <br/><b>Vinnsla</b> <br/>Góð PS vinna. Skerping upp á tíu og blurrið í hárinu kemur eðlilega út. Flottir litir, mjög eðlilegt allt saman. <br/> <br/><b>Titill og upplýsingar</b> <br/>Titillinn er einfaldur og lýsandi, rænir ekki athygli frá myndinni. Upplýsingarnar sem þú gefur upp innihalda allt þetta helsta, nema hvaða linsu þú notar. <br/> <br/><b>Niðurstaða (mitt álit)</b> <br/>Tæknilega er myndin mjög vel gerð. Kannski ekki mest frumleg í þessu þema en dettur þó ekki í neina klisju. Ef ég á að setja út á eitthvað myndi það vera skurðurinn við nefið. Hefði kannski líka prufað eitthvað annað í glasið, appelsínugulur er kannski full líkur húðlitnum. <br/> <br/>Kveðja, Halli</td>', '*Critique Club Comment* Magnað, ég hef ekki fengið random mynd í þessum CC klúbb í marga mánuði og þegar ég geri það dett ég niður á þína mynd.  Myndbygging Fín bygging í myndinni, flott að fylla rammann. Eina sem fer í taugarnar á mér er að það rétt sést í nebbaling hægra megin, hefði e.t.v. mátt croppa nær glasinu eða vel inn í það.  Lýsing Fín lýsing hjá þér, Bowens gaurinn að skila sínu. Fer ekki í taugarnar á mér að myndist "yfirlýst" svæði á glasinu. Húðin kemur vel út og aðalatriðið, augað, er mjög flott.  Tækni (e. camera work) Mjög fín, kann vel við DOF í myndinni, hárið hæfilega blurrað eins og glasið og augað súper skarpt.  Vinnsla Góð PS vinna. Skerping upp á tíu og blurrið í hárinu kemur eðlilega út. Flottir litir, mjög eðlilegt allt saman.  Titill og upplýsingar Titillinn er einfaldur og lýsandi, rænir ekki athygli frá myndinni. Upplýsingarnar sem þú gefur upp innihalda allt þetta helsta, nema hvaða linsu þú notar.  Niðurstaða (mitt álit) Tæknilega er myndin mjög vel gerð. Kannski ekki mest frumleg í þessu þema en dettur þó ekki í neina klisju. Ef ég á að setja út á eitthvað myndi það vera skurðurinn við nefið. Hefði kannski líka prufað eitthvað annað í glasið, appelsínugulur er kannski full líkur húðlitnum.  Kveðja, Halli', '2005-11-09 20:25:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852718, 251327, 7543, '<td valign="top"><br/>A bit too busy for my eyes. Framing of subjects is good, but there is just too much going on there. Colors are good, could use a bit more sharpening.  Can''t see how the mask feature comes into play, wish you had said more about that as I am curious.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - removed cc status.</i></td>', 'A bit too busy for my eyes. Framing of subjects is good, but there is just too much going on there. Colors are good, could use a bit more sharpening.  Can''t see how the mask feature comes into play, wish you had said more about that as I am curious.Message edited by HBunch - removed cc status.', '2005-11-09 20:34:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852824, 249507, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>What an interesting idea, great concept under the transparency category. I like that you were brave and put yourself out there, I think that is awesome.   The subject is great, I love the combining of two subject like a woman and science.  The first thing that hits me from the image is your shirt. The texture draws away from the subject just a little bit, I would have preferred a basic t-shirt either white or black.  Also there seems to be a little bit of a line above your head on the left side, some one commented on possible window sill in the shot.  I really love the warming feeling in the background color, it almost looks like a perfect backdrop.  I think you did a great job I would have scored you higher then what you received.  Good job.</td>', '*Critique Club* What an interesting idea, great concept under the transparency category. I like that you were brave and put yourself out there, I think that is awesome.   The subject is great, I love the combining of two subject like a woman and science.  The first thing that hits me from the image is your shirt. The texture draws away from the subject just a little bit, I would have preferred a basic t-shirt either white or black.  Also there seems to be a little bit of a line above your head on the left side, some one commented on possible window sill in the shot.  I really love the warming feeling in the background color, it almost looks like a perfect backdrop.  I think you did a great job I would have scored you higher then what you received.  Good job.', '2005-11-09 21:09:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852845, 251387, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Awesome job, this is a very well deserved finish.  This was an extremely tough category in my opinion.  It was really nice to see something different in this category as well.  I love the warm feel in such a  scenic farm town area.  I don''t see much of anything I would change.  The only thing, very small is possibly is adjust the contrast to make the sky a little bluer or add a polarizing filter.  You did an excellent job, congradulations!</td>', '*Critique Club* Awesome job, this is a very well deserved finish.  This was an extremely tough category in my opinion.  It was really nice to see something different in this category as well.  I love the warm feel in such a  scenic farm town area.  I don''t see much of anything I would change.  The only thing, very small is possibly is adjust the contrast to make the sky a little bluer or add a polarizing filter.  You did an excellent job, congradulations!', '2005-11-09 21:16:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852880, 251557, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>This is a great shot, I love the warm colors and shine.  The only thing is I like  a few others are not sure of what it is. I think I understand that we are looking through an amazing windshield that show no reflection and possibly looking at a boat. The little shine or stars are so prefect they look like they were placed on the image afterward.  Personally I think you scored lower on the image because people did not know what they were looking at, I know that is what I thought.  Its a bummer there is that one dark spot on the white space between the wiper and metal piece. Great angle and taking a different point of view from the basic run of the mill image.  Good job on making us all think and question what we are looking at.</td>', '*Critique Club* This is a great shot, I love the warm colors and shine.  The only thing is I like  a few others are not sure of what it is. I think I understand that we are looking through an amazing windshield that show no reflection and possibly looking at a boat. The little shine or stars are so prefect they look like they were placed on the image afterward.  Personally I think you scored lower on the image because people did not know what they were looking at, I know that is what I thought.  Its a bummer there is that one dark spot on the white space between the wiper and metal piece. Great angle and taking a different point of view from the basic run of the mill image.  Good job on making us all think and question what we are looking at.', '2005-11-09 21:28:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852920, 251435, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>What an eye catcher! I love the color.  I also think like the other commentator that the guy in the image is happy, maybe its the warm yellow color but you just feel like he is having a  good day. I really do also love the detail on the reflection, the rock and leaves are so great to see. The glow from what I think would be the clouds is so cool to see in the reflection as well.  I really don''t see anything I would recommend changing or altering.  Excellent job!</td>', '*Critique Club* What an eye catcher! I love the color.  I also think like the other commentator that the guy in the image is happy, maybe its the warm yellow color but you just feel like he is having a  good day. I really do also love the detail on the reflection, the rock and leaves are so great to see. The glow from what I think would be the clouds is so cool to see in the reflection as well.  I really don''t see anything I would recommend changing or altering.  Excellent job!', '2005-11-09 21:42:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(1852978, 251463, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>This is a ideal image for either the transparency contest or reflection.  What an amazing place to see able to take this shot, I would have never guessed that this was a museum. I love the placement of where you took the shot from as well as the composition.  The image seems so peaceful, possibly from all the blue. I also really like that you can sees a little sail boat out on the water as well as people outside the building. The only I would comment on is those small reflections in the windows in just a few small place.  Overall I think you did a great job, people may have just voted it higher in the reflection category.</td>', '*Critique Club* This is a ideal image for either the transparency contest or reflection.  What an amazing place to see able to take this shot, I would have never guessed that this was a museum. I love the placement of where you took the shot from as well as the composition.  The image seems so peaceful, possibly from all the blue. I also really like that you can sees a little sail boat out on the water as well as people outside the building. The only I would comment on is those small reflections in the windows in just a few small place.  Overall I think you did a great job, people may have just voted it higher in the reflection category.', '2005-11-09 22:04:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1853000, 250274, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Wow what a bright image! I think this is a prefect fit for the challenge and nice to see something different from the bunch. Excellent idea using oranges, other fruit would not have been nearly as bright or colorful. I think the composition of this shot is the best part.  The only thing I would have played with a little is the placement of the oranges, not quite so centered for the two, maybe off more the the left or right.  I am really glad you left the seeds in they add another element to the photo. The glow from the bottom orange is a nice complement to the the seeds and outside of the orange that are darker in the shot.</td>', '*Critique Club* Wow what a bright image! I think this is a prefect fit for the challenge and nice to see something different from the bunch. Excellent idea using oranges, other fruit would not have been nearly as bright or colorful. I think the composition of this shot is the best part.  The only thing I would have played with a little is the placement of the oranges, not quite so centered for the two, maybe off more the the left or right.  I am really glad you left the seeds in they add another element to the photo. The glow from the bottom orange is a nice complement to the the seeds and outside of the orange that are darker in the shot.', '2005-11-09 22:10:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1853252, 250983, 6477, '<td valign="top">Gary! you''re a genius!! :))<br/>I really love it... simply fantastic...</td>', 'Gary! you''re a genius!! :))I really love it... simply fantastic...', '2005-11-09 23:06:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(1853307, 249993, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi GentleSoul...<br/> <br/>I wouldn''t consider this photo to be a failure.  After reading your comments on it, I believe you learned a good bit about improvised lighting.  Whether the image is ''dull'' or not is somewhat subjective.  The image is the beginning of a good product style shot.  It''s very difficult to achieve white backgrounds without strobes, but it can be done.  Custom white balance settings help, and in the case of this photo, overexposing the photo by about one stop would help also.  <br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi GentleSoul... I wouldn''t consider this photo to be a failure.  After reading your comments on it, I believe you learned a good bit about improvised lighting.  Whether the image is ''dull'' or not is somewhat subjective.  The image is the beginning of a good product style shot.  It''s very difficult to achieve white backgrounds without strobes, but it can be done.  Custom white balance settings help, and in the case of this photo, overexposing the photo by about one stop would help also.   Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2005-11-09 23:20:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(1853441, 250983, 41802, '<td valign="top">Wow amazing job! So glad to see a ribon for you!</td>', 'Wow amazing job! So glad to see a ribon for you!', '2005-11-09 23:47:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(1854206, 250109, 37719, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I have studied your image and have the following to offer:<br/> <br/>Composition/perspective - Your choice of location was an interesting one - perhaps you didn''t have much choice, no way to know.  But this shot has so many elements to it that are pleasing and interesting.  Very full without being busy.  The scene behind the train I think makes it work well.  Placement of the train in the shot works nicely and helps to keep you focused - not much room to stray.  I like the balance between the apparent quiet and emptiness in the foreground separated from the busy night scene in the background by the train.  Your exposure length not only gives you the effect you were going for, but allows the background to develop a lot of great detail enhanced by the lights.  Framing of the train in relation to the surrounding wires and supports is well done.<br/> <br/>Color - for a night shot this has a very rich palette of colors that are all pleasing to the eye and work very well together.  The sky is such a nice blue without being too dark.  The stripes from the train colors add a great touch.  The flow from top to bottom - dark sky to the white of the snow is a very nice transition.<br/> <br/>Lighting - use of natural/available light in this shot could not be better.  Your time of day for this exposure was chosen well. Your length of exposure created really nice effects in the background lights.  This demonstrates an excellent command of your surroundings with the camera.  Well done!<br/> <br/>Challenge requirements - although your transparency is created by exposure, it still works and meets the challenge.  <br/> <br/>Overall/my opinion - this is an excellent image where all the elements come together very well.  My only suggestion would be try cropping so the electrical support column is a bit more off center.  Other than that you have an excellent image here that I think viewers just fell short on because of how you created your effect.  Very well done!</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! I have studied your image and have the following to offer: Composition/perspective - Your choice of location was an interesting one - perhaps you didn''t have much choice, no way to know.  But this shot has so many elements to it that are pleasing and interesting.  Very full without being busy.  The scene behind the train I think makes it work well.  Placement of the train in the shot works nicely and helps to keep you focused - not much room to stray.  I like the balance between the apparent quiet and emptiness in the foreground separated from the busy night scene in the background by the train.  Your exposure length not only gives you the effect you were going for, but allows the background to develop a lot of great detail enhanced by the lights.  Framing of the train in relation to the surrounding wires and supports is well done. Color - for a night shot this has a very rich palette of colors that are all pleasing to the eye and work very well together.  The sky is such a nice blue without being too dark.  The stripes from the train colors add a great touch.  The flow from top to bottom - dark sky to the white of the snow is a very nice transition. Lighting - use of natural/available light in this shot could not be better.  Your time of day for this exposure was chosen well. Your length of exposure created really nice effects in the background lights.  This demonstrates an excellent command of your surroundings with the camera.  Well done! Challenge requirements - although your transparency is created by exposure, it still works and meets the challenge.   Overall/my opinion - this is an excellent image where all the elements come together very well.  My only suggestion would be try cropping so the electrical support column is a bit more off center.  Other than that you have an excellent image here that I think viewers just fell short on because of how you created your effect.  Very well done!', '2005-11-10 08:59:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(1854271, 251580, 37719, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I have studied your image and have the following to offer:<br/> <br/>Composition/perspective - the ratio between subject and negative space in this image is nice.  Placement of subject is well done - nice rule of thirds application.  Head on and keeping it level is also good for this image.  The focus is clear on your subject, but the negative space seems fuzzy.  <br/> <br/>Color - a very narrow palette in this shot.  The brown has a very rich tonal quality and the grain in the wood helps to keep it from being bland.  Again, it would be stronger if in better focus on the negative space.  The lantern itself doesn''t have much to help it stand out against the background other than the flame.  It would have been nice to see the dark base and tarnished top more pronounced against the background.  Would have added a nice element of contrast.<br/> <br/>Lighting - I think this is one element where this image fell short in the challenge.  It seems the only source of light in the shot is the flame itself.  This makes the whole image dark.  Perhaps a bounced light  of a soft quality off the background would have helped this.  It also would have enhanced the quality of transparency.  Although you can see the color through the glass, there is no definition.  Again, a bounced light would have helped to bring this out.<br/> <br/>Challenge requirements - due to the darkness of the image (see above) and the fact that there really is not any defined element behind the glass, the transparency only goes so far as the flame.  I don''t think this is strong enough to carry the image by itself.  More depth would definitely have made this a much stronger entry.<br/> <br/>Overall/my opinion - this is a good idea and would have worked much better with different lighting.  You demonstrated good control of the low light and reflection of the flame in the glass.  A slightly taller flame would also have worked.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! I have studied your image and have the following to offer: Composition/perspective - the ratio between subject and negative space in this image is nice.  Placement of subject is well done - nice rule of thirds application.  Head on and keeping it level is also good for this image.  The focus is clear on your subject, but the negative space seems fuzzy.   Color - a very narrow palette in this shot.  The brown has a very rich tonal quality and the grain in the wood helps to keep it from being bland.  Again, it would be stronger if in better focus on the negative space.  The lantern itself doesn''t have much to help it stand out against the background other than the flame.  It would have been nice to see the dark base and tarnished top more pronounced against the background.  Would have added a nice element of contrast. Lighting - I think this is one element where this image fell short in the challenge.  It seems the only source of light in the shot is the flame itself.  This makes the whole image dark.  Perhaps a bounced light  of a soft quality off the background would have helped this.  It also would have enhanced the quality of transparency.  Although you can see the color through the glass, there is no definition.  Again, a bounced light would have helped to bring this out. Challenge requirements - due to the darkness of the image (see above) and the fact that there really is not any defined element behind the glass, the transparency only goes so far as the flame.  I don''t think this is strong enough to carry the image by itself.  More depth would definitely have made this a much stronger entry. Overall/my opinion - this is a good idea and would have worked much better with different lighting.  You demonstrated good control of the low light and reflection of the flame in the glass.  A slightly taller flame would also have worked.', '2005-11-10 09:47:38.000000', NULL, 0),
